WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.600
Agriculture has
a significant effect

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.000
on greenhouse gasses.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.000
What foods and diets are best
for reducing that impact?

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.345
Check it out.

00:00:10.501 --> 00:00:20.471
"Which Foods and Diets Have
the Lowest Carbon Footprint?"

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.920
Our eating habits are making us and
the planet increasingly unhealthy.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.920
Ours is a lose-lose situation;

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.940
a global transformation of the
food system is urgently needed.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:37.440
In consideration of the mounting evidence
regarding the environmental effects of

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.000
foods, for the 2015 to 2020 U.S. Dietary
Guidelines the scientific advisory

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.380
committee included for the first time
a chapter focused on food safety

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:51.240
and sustainability, concluding,
"a dietary pattern that is higher

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.160
in plant-based foods,
such as vegetables, fruits,

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.780
whole grains, legumes,
nuts, and seeds,

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:59.040
and lower in animal-based foods,
is not only more health promoting

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.220
but also associated with lesser
environmental impact..."

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:08.040
Despite unprecedented public support,
this and other sustainability language

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.640
was, not surprisingly, vanished
from the final Dietary Guidelines

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:15.780
published jointly by the U.S. Department
of Agriculture. They're not even

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.700
sufficiently sticking to the science on
healthy eating either, including no, or

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.660
too lax limits for animal-source foods,
despite the available evidence.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:29.760
Even if they ignored planetary health
altogether and just stuck to the latest

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.260
evidence on healthy eating, it would
have knock-on environmental benefits.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.740
Replacing animal-source foods with
plant-based ones would not only

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.960
improve nutrition and
help people live longer,

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.620
but could reduce greenhouse
gas emissions up to 84%.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.600
In general, plant-based foods cause
fewer adverse environmental effects

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:53.460
by nearly any measure. In terms of
carbon footprint, all the foods that are

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.540
the equivalent of driving more than a
mile per serving are animal products.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:01.380
Here's the greenhouse gas
emissions from various foods.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:05.280
Even though something like a lamb
chop or farmed fish may be the worst,

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.240
eating chicken still causes like
five times the global warming

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:14.040
than even something like tropical fruit,
though the climate superstars

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:17.640
are legumes—beans, split peas,
chickpeas, and lentils.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.480
For example, in the United States,
substituting beans for beef

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:27.240
at the national level could alone deliver
up to 75% of the 2020 greenhouse gas

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.620
emissions reduction target and
spare an area of land 1.5 times

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:36.480
the size of California, not to
mention the health benefits.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:40.980
And it's not just greenhouse gases.
Kidney beans required approximately

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:46.680
18 times less land, 10 times less water,
9 times less fuel, 12 times less fertilizer,

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:52.020
and 10 times less pesticides. So
yeah, according to the prestigious

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.960
EAT-Lancet Commission, more
plant-based may be better,

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:00.240
but even a shift towards a healthier
dietary pattern emphasizing whole grains,

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.940
fruits, vegetables, nuts, and
legumes without necessarily

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:06.120
eating strictly-plant-based,
would be beneficial.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.020
In Europe, for example, just halving the
consumption of meat, dairy, and eggs

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:15.960
would achieve up to a 40% reduction in
nitrogen emissions and greenhouse gas

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:20.940
emissions, and require about a fifth less
land. In addition, the dietary changes

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.240
would also lower health risks,
reducing cardiovascular mortality,

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:29.640
their leading cause of death.
Note, however, that minimizing

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:33.360
environmental impacts does not
necessarily maximize human health.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:38.280
Yes, animal products, dairy, eggs, fish,
and other meat releases significantly

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.580
more greenhouse gas per
serving than foods from plants;

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:47.160
eating added sugar and oil isn't going
to do your own body any favors.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:50.580
In California, including more animal
products in your diet requires

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:54.000
an additional 10,000
quarts of water a week.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.320
So that's like taking 150
more showers a week.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:02.880
Even just skipping meat on weekdays
could conserve thousands of gallons

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:06.240
a week compared to eating meat
every day, and cut your daily

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.380
carbon footprint and total
ecological footprint by about 40%.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.520
Some countries are actually
doing something about it.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.400
The Chinese government, for example,
has outlined a plan to reduce

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:21.840
its citizens' meat consumption by 50%,
whereas much of the rest of the world

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:25.140
appears to be doing the complete
opposite, pumping billions

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:29.100
of taxpayer dollars into subsidizing
the meat, dairy, and egg industries.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:35.700
We can certainly all try to do our part;
however, an obstacle to dietary change

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:39.540
may be consumers' underestimation
of the environmental impacts

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:43.440
of different types of food, but
may be aided by labeling.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:49.380
For example, imagine picking up a can
of a beef noodle soup and seeing this.

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:52.260
The carbon footprint of
a single half-cup serving

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.800
is like leaving a light on
for 39 hours straight.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:00.960
And not some eco-bulb, right? An
old-school 100-watt hot incandescent,

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:06.120
compared to a meat-free vegetable
soup—a difference of 34 light-bulb hours.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:11.940
You can imagine someone getting on your
case for unnecessarily leaving on a light

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:20.000
for 34 minutes, but this is 34 hours
just eating a different half cup of soup.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.360
In what “was arguably the largest
ever environmental protest

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:27.540
in the world,” more than a
million children, across more than

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:31.020
a hundred countries, joined
a “Global Climate March,

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:32.880
demanding that governments act…”

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.580
“The concerns of the young
protesters are justified”

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.680
and “supported by the
best available science,”

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:39.480
wrote a group of
scientists and scholars.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.780
The enormous mobilization shows
that young people have at least

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:46.020
understood the situation, and
“we strongly support their demand

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:47.880
for rapid and forceful action.”

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:51.960
In terms of our food supply there
are all sorts of little tweaks,

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:55.140
like feed additives that
can reduce cattle belching,

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:58.380
but put all those tweaks together,
according to the prestigious

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:00.360
EAT-Lancet Commission, and
we’re only talking about

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:03.420
reducing agricultural greenhouse-
gas emissions in 2050

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:07.380
by about 10% ; whereas, if
we instead switched over

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:11.340
to plant foods, we “could reduce
emissions by up to 80 percent.”

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:16.860
All those cow, sheep, and goat
burps only represent a fraction

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:20.400
of the greenhouse gas emissions
from animal agriculture;

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:24.180
so, that’s why according to the IPCC,
the official Intergovernmental

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:28.080
Panel on Climate Change, becoming
a so-called climate carnivore,

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:30.660
just cutting down on
ruminant products like beef,

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:33.960
wouldn’t reduce greenhouse
gas emissions as much

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:38.640
as eating a healthier diet and
limiting meat across the board.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:41.760
And, the fewer animal
products the better.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:46.320
“Which diet has the least environmental
impact on our planet?”

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:50.700
A systematic review found that
eating completely plant-based

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:53.040
may be “the optimal diet
for the environment.”

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:56.580
But it’s not all or nothing.
Even just cutting down on meat

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:59.400
to under an ounce or two
a day could get you half

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.640
the way there in terms of
reducing greenhouse gas emissions.

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:06.600
In terms of land use, a healthier
diet, like a Mediterranean diet,

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:10.260
may decrease your footprint by
about a quarter, whereas even more

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:14.280
plant-based diets can
drop land use 50% or more.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:18.120
In general, diets that
include meat require

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:19.920
about three times more water,

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:22.800
13 times more fertilizer,
more than twice the energy,

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:26.460
and 40% more pesticides
than those that don’t.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:30.180
If you look even broader at
the total environmental impact

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:34.140
of omnivorous versus
vegetarian versus vegan diets,

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:37.860
looking not just at global warming,
but ocean acidification,

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:42.660
agricultural run-off, smog,
ecotoxicity of the water and soil,

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:45.532
and direct human toxicity
of the air we breathe

00:07:45.532 --> 00:07:48.720
and the water we drink, and
the soil we grow our food from,

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:53.280
eating eggs and dairy may be
nine times worse than plants.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:57.060
And, eating eggs, dairy, and
meat may be 17 times worse

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:59.220
than sticking to plant foods.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:04.980
Oh, and as a bonus, we could feed
an additional 350 million Americans,

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:08.160
like an entire extra
country’s worth of people—

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:11.640
more than if we eliminated
food waste completely.

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:16.020
Changing meat-eating habits
would seem to be a relatively

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:18.420
cheap and easy way to
mitigate climate change,

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:21.300
in contrast to many other
factors outside our control.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:25.620
However, surveys suggest few
seem to recognize this option

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:29.700
of eating less meat as a
signiﬁcant opportunity for helping.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:33.000
Research has shown that
consumers often underestimate

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.580
the impacts of meat consumption
on the environment in general,

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:38.040
and on climate change in particular.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:42.660
“The outstanding effectiveness
of the less meat option

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:45.720
(as established by climate
experts) was recognized by…

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:49.620
[only] 6% of [Americans sampled,]
and that’s after they were

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:53.460
prompted to assume climate
change is actually happening.

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:56.700
“There is an overwhelming
scientific consensus that

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:59.400
climate change is real and
that we’re driving it,”

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:03.300
but only about half of
U.S. adults believe it.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:07.500
This is not by coincidence.
Just like the tobacco industry

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:10.380
tried to subvert the
overwhelming evidence that

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:14.100
smoking caused cancer, companies
like “Exxon orchestrated

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.800
climate change denial
campaign[s] that stalled

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:19.200
meaningful efforts… for decades.”

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:23.460
Certainly, environmental groups
should know better, though.

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:28.560
“None of the highest profile
NGOs examined” appeared to want

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:32.040
to feature the link between meat
consumption and climate change.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:34.440
They were all aware of
the evidence, of course,

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:37.620
but evidently the science
alone was not sufﬁcient.

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:41.160
It’s like another form of
denialism that can become like

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:45.720
a negative feedback loop, where
it’s not popular to talk about,

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:48.840
so you don’t talk about it,
so it remains not popular

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:51.330
to talk about, depriving
the issue of the attention

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:53.280
that it needs to break out.

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:57.000
And when they have messaged
about it, environmental groups

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:01.020
have tended to favor just
asking for a moderate reduction

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:04.200
in meat consumption,
notable given the research

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:07.140
demonstrating how much more
powerful a lever it could be

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:10.560
at the individual level
to go even further.

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:14.580
But, they don’t want to be seen
as telling people what to do,

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:19.380
instead advocating for small
changes, like turning off

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:23.340
your computer monitor at
lunchtime or printing double-sided.

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:27.000
But, “the cumulative impact of
large numbers of individuals

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:30.060
making [just] marginal improvements
in their environmental impact”

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:34.440
may end up constituting just a
marginal collective improvement.

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:37.680
Yet, we now live at a
time when we need to make

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:41.166
urgent and ambitious changes.

